{"title":"The Mariner","logline":"In a collapsed near-future city ruled by gangs, a hulking Newfoundland vigilante who models himself on his idealized fisherman father storms a mob-held skyscraper to rescue his sister — only to learn that the hero who forged him never existed, forcing him to decide whether he can be a hero on his own terms.","synopsis":"In a near-future urban wasteland — hollowed out by the pandemic and the financial collapse of 2025 — vigilantes have replaced the police. THE MARINER, a mountainous man in fishing-net cape, bandolier of shotgun shells, and armed with a metal-edged oar carved 'BOSUN', assaults the Ruddy & Greene building where his sister ROSE is being held by the Salvatori crime family. He fights his way to the top floor, extracts her, and they retreat into the elevator, blood-spattered and bickering. His rescue method: an AirTag he secretly hid in her purse at Thanksgiving.

Rose refuses to leave without the purse — it holds a chip with the blockchain password to twenty million dollars she stole from the gang, egged on by her boyfriend Vinnie, one of Salvatori's men. Against his 'Mariner's code' of honesty, bravery and grit, Billy follows her back up through the building. They batter through thugs on the stairwell, the 13th floor (where he flare-guns Vinnie and cracks two heads together, taking a bullet in the leg), and reach Salvatori's luxurious 15th-floor office, where the purse waits on the desk.

There, in the lull before the ambush, Rose detonates the film's real bomb: their father isn't dead. He's alive in Chimney Bay, a deadbeat drunk who beat Billy, dumped him in a friend's yard for a summer, and abandoned the family. Flashbacks curdle from golden-lit fishing lessons and encouraging bench presses into a weed-choked yard where Dad punches his son in the face and calls him 'a bag a sad.' Salvatori arrives with a submachine gun and demands the password; asked if he's a big hero, the gutted Mariner kneels and says 'No.' Rose then delivers the counter-argument: the hero isn't the father — it's the boy who survived him and built something better. As Salvatori screams for the password, Mariner smiles and clenches his fists. Cut to black.","act_structure":[{"act_number":1,"title":"Setup — The Rescue","start_scene":"EXT. CITY CENTRE - NIGHT (ruined city, opening title crawl)","end_scene":"INT. RUDDY & GREEN BUILDING - ELEVATOR — Rose asks 'You think they're after you?' as the doors open on 12","summary":"The Mariner storms a mob building offscreen and extracts his sister Rose, establishing the ruined world, his absurd nautical vigilante persona, and his protective fixation on family.","turning_points":["Opening title establishes post-collapse city where vigilantes replace police","Mariner's entire assault is heard, not seen — building floors light up one by one","Reveal of the Mariner's costume and blood-slick oar in the elevator","AirTag reveal: he has been secretly tracking his sister","Rose's laugh — 'You think they're after you?' — flips the premise; she is the target, not him"]},{"act_number":2,"title":"Confrontation — Back Up the Building","start_scene":"Elevator doors open on 12; three thugs waiting","end_scene":"INT. 15TH FLOOR — Mariner splinters the office door open and Rose retrieves her purse","summary":"Rose forces a return trip up the tower for a purse containing the blockchain key to twenty million dollars she stole from the Salvatori gang, and brother and sister fight floor by floor while their competing versions of morality collide.","turning_points":["Elevator ambush on 12; Rose picks 11 to take the stairs 'because they won't expect it'","First flashback: idealized father teaching young Billy to fish and defining a hero","Rosco interrogation reveals the theft is worth 20 million of gang money","Mariner's code stated aloud — 'honesty, bravery, and grit... a true hero never backs down' — and Rose's retort, 'You know who you sound like'","13th floor firefight: Mariner flare-guns ex-boyfriend Vinnie, is shot in the leg, kills Mikey and Carlos; 'BOSUN' carved in the oar revealed","Reaching Salvatori's untouched luxury floor and breaking into the office"]},{"act_number":3,"title":"Resolution — The Truth and the Choice","start_scene":"INT. 15TH FLOOR OFFICE — Rose: 'That's gotta be juice, right?'","end_scene":"Mariner smiles and clenches his fists as Salvatori screams for the password; CUT TO BLACK","summary":"Rose reveals their father is alive, a drunk and abusive deadbeat, collapsing the myth the Mariner built himself on; Salvatori arrives and the broken hero kneels, until Rose rebuilds him with a truer origin story.","turning_points":["Rose reveals Dad isn't dead — he's in Chimney Bay, was on the pogey, abandoned Billy","Flashbacks curdle: the same backyard and boat scenes replayed as abuse and neglect","Salvatori's entrance with submachine gun; thugs disarm Mariner and force him to his knees","'Are you a big fucking hero?' — 'No.' The hero's total collapse","Rose's speech reframes the hero as Billy himself, who 'turned a deadbeat father into a superhero, then became a real one'","Mariner clenches his fists — self-authored heroism replaces inherited myth"]}],"themes":[{"theme":"The collapse of an origin myth","description":"The Mariner's entire identity — costume, code, oar, nickname 'Bosun' — is an homage to a father who was a fantasy. When the myth crumbles, so does the hero, and the story asks whether a heroism built on a lie can survive the truth.","evidence":["'It's functional! ... And an homage.'","'It broke me when he died.' / 'Died!?'","Golden-lit fishing flashback later replayed with Dad pissing off the side of the boat and the child shivering","Salvatori: 'Are you a big fucking hero?' — Mariner: 'No.'"]},{"theme":"Inherited abuse versus chosen character","description":"Dad's lessons ('don't never run from a fight,' 'it's not what a MacAngus does') were delivered with fists. The script argues Billy's virtue is not descended from his father but was forged in spite of him.","evidence":["Backyard flashback: Dad punches young Mariner, 'Yer a bag a sad. Go lift some tin till yer a man.'","'He grew up in a shitty home with a shitty father... And instead of following that path, he forged his own.'","'You know who you sound like...' after Mariner recites his code","The 'Dad' heart-and-arrow tattoo he keeps looking at mid-fight"]},{"theme":"Family loyalty and its limits","description":"Billy's love for Rose is invasive (the AirTag) but total; Rose's love for Billy has protected him with a lie and now wounds him with the truth. Their bickering sibling dynamic carries the whole film.","evidence":["'I stashed an AirTag in your purse. Last Thanksgiving at ma's.'","'Who got you involved, Mr. Fucking AirTag?'","'You were too young, Will. She didn't want to break your heart.'","Rose's final speech, spoken with a gun at her head, to save her brother's self-image"]},{"theme":"Codes of honour in a lawless world","description":"In a city where police have withdrawn and vigilantes fill the gap, the Mariner clings to a rigid personal code — honesty, bravery, grit — that Rose's pragmatic theft directly violates.","evidence":["Opening title: 'Vigilanties arise to do what the police couldn't.'","'I don't steal, Rose. It's against my code.'","'From criminals? We take it, their gang collapses. And what code??'","Mariner reluctantly returning up the tower, breaking his own rule for his sister"]},{"theme":"Absurdity as armour","description":"The nautical costume, flare gun, fish-truck, and elevator muzak play as comedy, but every ridiculous element is a load-bearing piece of the fantasy that keeps a beaten child upright.","evidence":["Muzak 'Faith' by George Michael scoring an offscreen massacre","'Hey, Finding Nemo, drop the piece.' / 'Is that a... flare gun?'","'McAngus Fish & Brewis' delivery van with a giant fake fish on top","The oar carved with 'BOSUN' — his father's nickname for him — used as a weapon"]}],"narrative_arc":"A compressed, single-location vertical thriller: a rescue that appears complete in the first five minutes is immediately reversed, sending the protagonist back up through the building he has already conquered. The physical ascent (elevator, 11th, 12th, 13th, 15th floors) is mirrored by a psychological descent, as flashbacks that begin as golden-lit idylls are progressively revealed as abuse. The action climax and the emotional climax invert each other: the Mariner is disarmed and kneeling at the moment of maximum threat, at his lowest, and is resurrected not by force but by his sister's words. The script ends on an unresolved cliffhanger — fists clenched, cut to black — withholding the fight to keep the transformation, not the violence, as the payoff.","genre":"Action-Comedy / Superhero Drama","tone":"Darkly comedic and hyper-violent on the surface, with a raw, melancholy emotional core; blue-collar Newfoundland vernacular and bathetic humour undercutting genuine trauma","protagonist_journey":"Billy 'The Mariner' MacAngus begins as a self-invented folk hero performing a dead father's imagined virtues — costume, code, and oar all monuments to a man he mourns. Forced by his sister into a crime that violates his code, he is stripped step by step: shot, disarmed, forced to his knees, and finally told his father is alive, abusive, and worthless. Confronted by Salvatori he denies himself entirely ('Nobody... No'), the total collapse of his identity. Rose's speech offers a new origin — not inherited heroism but self-made survival — and his closing smile and clenched fists mark the shift from imitating a hero to being one on his own authority.","central_conflict":"Externally, the Mariner and Rose must survive Salvatori's gang and escape with the stolen blockchain key to twenty million dollars. Internally and more decisively, the Mariner must reconcile the heroic father-myth that built him with the truth that his father was an abusive deadbeat — and decide whether his heroism can stand without that foundation.","setting_overview":"A near-future North American city (Canadian-coded: Tim Hortons uniforms, 'pogey,' Newfoundland dialect, Chimney Bay), roughly the late 2020s. The 2020 pandemic emptied the skyscrapers; cascading bank failures and the 2025 commercial real estate collapse turned downtown cores into lawless no-go zones of burning barrels, rubble-choked intersections, and gunfire, where vigilantes have replaced absent police. Nearly the entire story unfolds inside the Ruddy & Greene Building, a bullet-scarred 15-storey sandstone tower — ruined, flickering floors below, an untouched nightclub-luxe penthouse above where the Salvatori gang operates. Flashbacks contrast an idyllic coastline and sunlit backyard with the same locations rendered squalid: beer bottles, rusty weights, a duct-taped bench press.","confidence":0.92}
